AI Vocabulary & Definitions
1. AI Fundamentals
Term	Simple Definition
Artificial Intelligence (AI)	Technology that enables computers to perform tasks that normally require human intelligence.
Machine Learning (ML)	A branch of AI where systems learn patterns from data instead of being explicitly programmed for every rule.
Deep Learning (DL)	ML using neural networks with many layers to learn complex patterns.
Neural Network	A computational model inspired loosely by the brain, made of interconnected nodes/neurons.
Algorithm	A defined procedure used to solve a problem or perform a computation.
Model	A trained computational system that makes predictions, classifications, or generates outputs.
Training	The process of teaching a model using data.
Inference	Using a trained model to produce an output from new input.
Dataset	A collection of data used for training, validation, or testing.
Feature	An input characteristic used by a machine-learning model.
Label	The expected answer associated with training data in supervised learning.
Prediction	The output generated by a model for an input.
Classification	Predicting which category an input belongs to.
Regression	Predicting a numerical value.
Clustering	Grouping similar data points without predefined labels.
Supervised Learning	ML where the training data contains known answers/labels.
Unsupervised Learning	ML where the model discovers patterns without labeled answers.
Reinforcement Learning (RL)	Learning through actions, rewards, and penalties.
Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF)	Improving a model using human preferences as feedback.
Reward Model	A model that evaluates outputs and provides a reward signal.
2. Generative AI
Term	Simple Definition
Generative AI (GenAI)	AI capable of generating new content such as text, images, audio, video, or code.
Foundation Model	A large pretrained model that can be adapted for many downstream tasks.
Large Language Model (LLM)	A model trained on large amounts of text to understand and generate language.
Multimodal Model	A model that can work with multiple data types such as text, images, audio, and video.
Generative Model	A model that learns patterns in data and generates new content based on them.
Diffusion Model	A generative model commonly used for image, video, and audio generation.
GAN	Generative Adversarial Network; two neural networks compete to generate realistic data.
Transformer	Neural-network architecture that became the foundation of most modern LLMs.
GPT	Generative Pre-trained Transformer. A family of transformer-based generative models.
Open-weight Model	A model whose trained weights are publicly available, often allowing self-hosting or modification.
Closed Model	A model whose underlying weights are not publicly available and is typically accessed through an API.
3. LLM Vocabulary
Term	Simple Definition
Token	A unit of text processed by an LLM. It can be a word, part of a word, punctuation, etc.
Tokenization	Converting text into tokens that the model can process.
Token Limit / Context Window	Maximum amount of information a model can process in one request/context.
Context	Information provided to the model to help it generate an answer.
Context Window	The total amount of input/output information the model can handle within a conversation/request.
Embedding	A numerical representation of data that captures semantic meaning.
Vector	A list of numbers representing information mathematically.
Vector Embedding	Numerical representation of text, image, audio, etc., used for similarity/search.
Attention	Mechanism allowing a model to determine which parts of the input are important to each other.
Self-Attention	Attention where tokens determine their relationships with other tokens in the same sequence.
Transformer	Architecture based heavily on attention mechanisms.
Parameters	Learned numerical values inside a model.
Weights	Learned parameters that determine how a neural network behaves.
Bias	Additional learned parameters that shift model calculations.
Temperature	Controls randomness/variability in generated output.
Top-K	Limits token selection to the K most probable candidates.
Top-P	Selects from the smallest group of tokens whose combined probability reaches P.
Logits	Raw numerical scores produced by a model before probabilities are calculated.
Probability Distribution	Probabilities assigned to possible next tokens.
Sampling	Selecting tokens based on their probability distribution.
Hallucination	When an AI generates information that appears plausible but is incorrect or unsupported.
Grounding	Connecting AI responses to trusted external information or data.
4. Prompt Engineering
Term	Simple Definition
Prompt	Instructions or input given to an AI model.
Prompt Engineering	Designing prompts to produce desired model behavior/output.
System Prompt	High-level instructions that define how a model should behave.
User Prompt	Instructions or questions provided by the user.
Instruction	A specific task or behavior requested from the model.
Few-shot Prompting	Providing a few examples to show the model what you expect.
One-shot Prompting	Providing one example.
Zero-shot Prompting	Asking the model to perform a task without examples.
Chain-of-Thought	A reasoning approach where intermediate reasoning is used to solve complex problems.
Prompt Template	Reusable prompt structure containing variable fields.
Prompt Chaining	Connecting multiple model calls where one output feeds another step.
Structured Output	Requiring the model to return information in a defined structure such as JSON.
Function Calling	Allowing a model to request execution of a predefined function/tool.
Tool Calling	Model-generated requests to external tools or systems.
Prompt Injection	Malicious or unintended instructions inserted into model input to alter behavior.
Jailbreak	Attempt to bypass a model's safety or behavioral restrictions.
5. RAG — Retrieval-Augmented Generation

This is very important for enterprise AI architecture.

Term	Definition
RAG	Retrieval-Augmented Generation; retrieves relevant information and provides it to an LLM before generating an answer.
Retriever	Component that searches for relevant information.
Retrieval	Finding relevant information from a knowledge source.
Knowledge Base	Collection of information used by an AI application.
Document Loader	Component that imports documents into an AI pipeline.
Chunking	Breaking large documents into smaller pieces.
Chunk	A smaller section of a document used for retrieval.
Chunk Size	Amount of text contained in each chunk.
Chunk Overlap	Amount of repeated text between neighboring chunks.
Embedding Model	Model that converts content into vectors.
Vector Database	Database optimized for storing and searching embeddings.
Semantic Search	Search based on meaning rather than exact keywords.
Similarity Search	Finding vectors mathematically similar to a query vector.
Metadata	Additional information attached to a document/chunk, such as author, date, or department.
Metadata Filtering	Restricting retrieval based on metadata.
Hybrid Search	Combining keyword search and semantic/vector search.
Reranking	Reordering retrieved results based on relevance.
Grounded Response	AI response based on retrieved source information.
Citation	Reference showing where retrieved information came from.
Retrieval Precision	How many retrieved results are actually relevant.
Retrieval Recall	How much of the relevant information was successfully retrieved.
Typical RAG architecture

User → Query → Embedding → Vector DB → Retrieval → Reranker → Context → LLM → Response

6. Fine-Tuning & Model Adaptation
Term	Definition
Fine-tuning	Further training a pretrained model on a specific dataset/task.
Pretraining	Initial large-scale training of a model on broad datasets.
Instruction Tuning	Training a model to better follow human instructions.
Domain Adaptation	Adapting a model to a specific industry/domain.
LoRA	Low-Rank Adaptation; efficient technique for fine-tuning models with fewer trainable parameters.
QLoRA	LoRA combined with quantization to reduce memory requirements.
Adapter	Small set of learned parameters added to a pretrained model.
Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT)	Fine-tuning methods that modify only a small portion of model parameters.
Catastrophic Forgetting	When fine-tuning causes a model to lose capabilities learned previously.
Training Dataset	Data used to train the model.
Validation Dataset	Data used during training to evaluate generalization.
Test Dataset	Data used for final model evaluation.
Epoch	One complete pass through the training dataset.
Batch	Group of training examples processed together.
Batch Size	Number of examples processed in one training step.
Learning Rate	Controls how quickly model parameters are updated during training.
Loss Function	Measures how far the model's prediction is from the desired result.
Gradient	Direction indicating how model parameters should change to reduce loss.
Backpropagation	Algorithm used to calculate gradients during neural-network training.
7. Quantization & Model Optimization

Especially relevant to your local LLM/Ollama/Mac work.

Term	Definition
Quantization	Reducing numerical precision of model weights/activations to reduce memory and computation.
FP32	32-bit floating-point representation.
FP16	16-bit floating-point representation.
BF16	Brain Floating Point 16-bit format commonly used for AI training/inference.
INT8	8-bit integer representation.
INT4	4-bit integer representation.
Q8	Approximately 8-bit quantized model representation.
Q6	Approximately 6-bit quantized representation.
Q4	Approximately 4-bit quantized representation.
Q3	Approximately 3-bit quantized representation.
MXFP4	Microscaling 4-bit floating-point format used for efficient AI computation.
Pruning	Removing less-important model parameters.
Distillation	Training a smaller model to reproduce the behavior of a larger model.
Knowledge Distillation	Transferring knowledge from a teacher model to a student model.
Model Compression	Techniques used to reduce model size and computational requirements.
8. AI Agents

This is one of the most important modern AI vocabulary areas.

Term	Definition
AI Agent	AI system capable of reasoning/planning and taking actions to achieve a goal.
Agentic AI	AI systems designed to autonomously perform multi-step tasks.
Agentic Workflow	Workflow where an AI model decides or executes multiple steps dynamically.
Agent Loop	Repeated cycle of reasoning → action → observation → next action.
Planner	Agent component responsible for determining what actions should be taken.
Executor	Component that performs planned actions.
Tool	External capability an AI agent can invoke.
Tool Calling	Model requesting an external tool/function to execute an action.
Memory	Information retained by an agent for future interactions.
Short-Term Memory	Information available during the current task/session.
Long-Term Memory	Information retained across sessions.
Reflection	Agent reviewing its own output or actions to improve results.
Planning	Breaking a goal into smaller executable steps.
Multi-Agent System	Multiple AI agents collaborating to accomplish a task.
Human-in-the-Loop (HITL)	Human approval or intervention within an AI workflow.
Autonomous Agent	Agent capable of executing tasks with minimal human intervention.
Agent Orchestration	Coordinating agents, tools, workflows, and models.
9. AI Architecture
Term	Definition
Inference Engine	System that executes a trained model to generate predictions/output.
Inference Server	Service that exposes a model for inference requests.
Model Serving	Making an AI model available to applications/users.
Model Gateway	Central service routing requests to different AI models.
AI Gateway	Gateway managing access, security, routing, monitoring, and policies for AI services.
Model Router	Component that selects which model should handle a request.
Orchestrator	Component coordinating models, tools, APIs, and workflows.
AI Pipeline	Sequence of processes used to prepare data, invoke models, and produce results.
Feature Store	Centralized system for storing and serving ML features.
Model Registry	Repository for tracking and managing ML models and versions.
Data Pipeline	Automated flow that collects, transforms, and delivers data.
Inference Pipeline	Workflow executed when a model processes new data.
Training Pipeline	Automated workflow for training and validating models.
MLOps	Practices for developing, deploying, monitoring, and maintaining ML systems.
LLMOps	Operational practices specifically for LLM applications.
AIOps	Use of AI/ML to automate IT operations and monitoring.
10. AI Infrastructure
Term	Definition
GPU	Processor highly suited for parallel AI computation.
CPU	General-purpose processor used for many computing workloads.
TPU	Google's specialized processor designed for machine-learning workloads.
NPU	Neural Processing Unit optimized for AI workloads.
CUDA	NVIDIA's platform/API ecosystem for GPU computing.
GPU Cluster	Multiple GPUs working together for AI workloads.
Distributed Training	Training a model across multiple machines/GPUs.
Model Parallelism	Splitting a model across multiple GPUs/devices.
Data Parallelism	Training replicas of a model on different data batches across devices.
GPU Memory / VRAM	Memory available to a GPU.
Inference Latency	Time required to generate an AI response.
Throughput	Number of requests/tokens processed over a period.
Tokens Per Second (TPS)	Number of tokens generated or processed per second.
Batch Inference	Processing many inference requests together.
Edge AI	Running AI inference close to where data is generated.
On-device AI	Running AI directly on a device rather than in the cloud.
11. AI Evaluation
Term	Definition
AI Evaluation / Eval	Measuring how well an AI system performs.
Benchmark	Standardized test used to compare model capabilities.
Accuracy	Percentage of predictions that are correct.
Precision	Percentage of predicted positives that are actually positive.
Recall	Percentage of actual positives correctly identified.
F1 Score	Combined measure of precision and recall.
BLEU	Metric historically used to evaluate machine translation.
ROUGE	Metric commonly used to evaluate text summarization.
Perplexity	Measure of how well a language model predicts text.
Human Evaluation	Humans assess the quality of AI outputs.
LLM-as-a-Judge	Using an LLM to evaluate another model's output.
Groundedness	Degree to which an answer is supported by provided information.
Faithfulness	Whether generated content accurately reflects its source/context.
Toxicity	Degree of harmful/offensive content in an output.
Robustness	Ability to perform reliably under varying conditions.
12. AI Security

For your network/security + AI architecture background, this area is particularly important.

Term	Definition
AI Security	Protecting AI models, data, infrastructure, and applications from threats.
Prompt Injection	Manipulating an AI through malicious instructions in input/context.
Indirect Prompt Injection	Malicious instructions hidden inside external content retrieved by an AI system.
Data Poisoning	Injecting malicious/corrupted data into training or knowledge sources.
Model Poisoning	Manipulating training/model components to alter model behavior.
Model Extraction	Attempting to reproduce a model by querying it repeatedly.
Model Inversion	Attempting to recover information about training data from model behavior.
Membership Inference	Determining whether specific data was used during model training.
Adversarial Example	Input intentionally modified to cause incorrect model behavior.
Jailbreaking	Attempting to bypass model safety controls.
Data Leakage	Unauthorized exposure of sensitive information.
PII	Personally Identifiable Information.
DLP	Data Loss Prevention; controls designed to prevent sensitive-data leakage.
Guardrail	Control that restricts or validates AI input/output/behavior.
Content Filtering	Detecting and blocking unwanted content.
AI Red Teaming	Deliberately testing AI systems for vulnerabilities and unsafe behavior.
Model Supply Chain Security	Securing models, datasets, dependencies, and artifacts used in AI systems.
13. Responsible AI
Term	Definition
Responsible AI	Designing and operating AI in a safe, fair, transparent, and accountable way.
AI Governance	Policies, processes, controls, and responsibilities governing AI use.
AI Ethics	Principles concerning appropriate and responsible AI development/use.
Bias	Systematic tendency that can produce unfair or skewed outcomes.
Fairness	Designing systems to avoid unjustified disparities between groups.
Explainability	Ability to explain how an AI system produced an outcome.
Interpretability	Degree to which humans can understand model behavior.
Transparency	Providing information about how an AI system works and is used.
Accountability	Clearly assigning responsibility for AI decisions and outcomes.
Privacy	Protecting personal or sensitive information used by AI systems.
AI Risk Management	Identifying, assessing, mitigating, and monitoring AI risks.
Model Card	Documentation describing a model's capabilities, limitations, and intended use.
Datasheet	Documentation describing a dataset's characteristics and intended use.
14. Enterprise AI
Term	Definition
Enterprise AI	AI designed for organizational/business use.
AI Transformation	Using AI to fundamentally change business processes and operations.
AI Copilot	AI assistant that helps a human perform tasks.
AI Assistant	AI system that responds to user requests and helps accomplish tasks.
AI Automation	Using AI to automate tasks or workflows.
AI-Augmented Workforce	Employees whose capabilities are enhanced by AI.
Enterprise Knowledge Base	Organization-specific information made available to AI applications.
Private AI	AI infrastructure designed to keep data/model processing within controlled environments.
Sovereign AI	AI infrastructure/data/models operated under a country's or jurisdiction's control.
AI Factory	Infrastructure/platform designed to develop, train, deploy, and operate AI workloads at scale.
AI Platform	Shared enterprise platform providing models, data, tools, governance, and infrastructure for AI applications.
AI Center of Excellence (AI CoE)	Team responsible for AI strategy, standards, governance, and enablement.
15. AI Cost & Performance
Term	Definition
Inference Cost	Cost of running a model to generate outputs.
Training Cost	Cost associated with training a model.
Token Cost	Cost calculated based on tokens processed/generated.
Latency	Time taken to produce a response.
Throughput	Amount of work processed per unit of time.
Context Cost	Additional inference cost associated with processing larger context.
Cost per Request	Average cost of processing one AI request.
Model Routing	Sending requests to different models based on complexity, cost, or requirements.
Small Language Model (SLM)	Smaller model optimized for lower cost/resource requirements.
Large Language Model (LLM)	Large model capable of broad language understanding/generation.
Model Cascading	Using a cheaper/smaller model first and escalating to a larger model when needed.
16. Multimodal AI
Term	Definition
Multimodal AI	AI capable of processing/generating multiple types of data.
Computer Vision	AI technology for understanding images and video.
OCR	Optical Character Recognition; extracting text from images/documents.
Speech Recognition	Converting spoken language into text.
Text-to-Speech (TTS)	Converting text into spoken audio.
Speech-to-Text (STT)	Converting speech into text.
Vision-Language Model (VLM)	Model that understands both visual and textual information.
Image Generation	Creating images using AI.
Video Generation	Creating or modifying video using AI.
Audio Generation	Creating speech, music, or other audio using AI.
17. AI Data Vocabulary
Term	Definition
Structured Data	Data organized into predefined fields/tables.
Unstructured Data	Data such as documents, images, audio, and video without a fixed table structure.
Semi-structured Data	Data with some organizational structure, such as JSON or XML.
Data Lake	Large repository for storing raw data.
Data Warehouse	System optimized for structured analytical data.
Data Lakehouse	Architecture combining characteristics of data lakes and warehouses.
Data Pipeline	Process that moves and transforms data.
Data Lineage	Tracking where data came from and how it changed.
Data Quality	Degree to which data is accurate, complete, consistent, and reliable.
Synthetic Data	Artificially generated data designed to resemble real data.
Knowledge Graph	Graph representing entities and relationships between them.
Graph RAG	RAG architecture that uses graph relationships to improve retrieval and reasoning.
18. Modern AI / LLM Architecture Terms

These are increasingly important in 2026 AI architecture discussions.

Term	Definition
Reasoning Model	Model optimized for solving complex multi-step problems.
Test-Time Compute	Additional computation performed during inference to improve reasoning.
Inference-Time Scaling	Increasing computation during inference rather than increasing model training.
Mixture of Experts (MoE)	Architecture where only selected expert networks process each input.
Expert	Specialized neural-network component within an MoE model.
Sparse Model	Model where only a subset of parameters are activated for each input.
Dense Model	Model where most/all parameters participate for each input.
Long-Context Model	Model capable of processing very large amounts of context.
Model Context Protocol (MCP)	Protocol for connecting AI models/agents with external tools and data sources.
Agent-to-Agent (A2A)	Communication/interoperability approach allowing AI agents to interact with other agents.
Computer Use	AI capability to interact with software interfaces like a human user.
Tool Use	Model's ability to invoke external functions/services.
Workflow Agent	Agent operating within a predefined workflow.
Autonomous Agent	Agent that can dynamically plan and execute actions toward a goal.
